  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Larger Pygmy Mole Grasshopper (Neotridactylus apicialis) is a small, cryptic insect belonging to the Pygmy Mole Cricket family, characterized by its burrowing lifestyle and reduced wings. Found throughout New England, this species is adapted to soil and leaf litter environments where it feeds on plant material and organic debris.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reduced Wing Structure",
      "description": "Wings are significantly shortened or vestigial, distinguishing it from typical grasshoppers with full-length wings",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Body Size",
      "description": "Measures only 8-12 mm in length, making it one of the smallest grasshoppers in the region",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Soil-Adapted Morphology",
      "description": "Features compact body, strong front legs modified for digging, and brownish coloration for camouflage in soil",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species inhabits moist soils, leaf litter, and decomposing organic matter in woodlands, gardens, and forest floors throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called a grasshopper, this creature is more closely related to crickets and has adapted to a subterranean lifestyle, rarely seen above ground and communicating through vibrations in the soil rather than audible chirps.",
